Example with Lambda

This is a very common pattern:

CloudWatch Alarm
      ↓
     SQS
      ↓
    Lambda
      ↓
Process message automatically

Lambda can be configured to automatically poll the SQS queue. When messages are available, Lambda invokes your function to process them.

The key difference

SNS:

Message → Push to subscribers

SQS:

Message → Wait in queue → Consumer retrieves it → Process → Delete

So remember:

SNS = "Tell someone/something now."
SQS = "Keep this message until a consumer processes it."






SNS vs SQS — when to use?
🔔 Use SNS when you want to notify

SNS is best when one message needs to be pushed to one or many subscribers.

Example: EC2 CPU becomes high.

EC2
 ↓
CloudWatch Alarm
 ↓
SNS
 ↓
 ├── Email → Admin 1
 ├── Email → Admin 2
 ├── Email → Admin 3
 └── Lambda

Use SNS for:

Email/SMS notifications
Alerts
Sending the same event to multiple services
Fan-out messaging

Memory:

SNS = Notify

📥 Use SQS when you want to queue work

SQS is best when you want to store messages until an application/worker is ready to process them.

Example: An e-commerce application receives 10,000 orders.

Users
 ↓
Application
 ↓
SQS Queue
 ↓
Worker
 ↓
Process orders

The worker can process the messages automatically.

Use SQS for:

Asynchronous processing
Handling large numbers of messages
Decoupling applications
Making sure messages aren't lost while waiting to be processed
Buffering traffic

Memory:

SQS = Queue and process

SNS + SQS together

Sometimes you need both.

Example:

"When an order is created, notify different systems and also make sure the order is processed."

                 Application
                      ↓
                     SNS
                  /       \
                 ↓         ↓
              Email       SQS
                           ↓
                        Worker
                           ↓
                    Process order

SNS broadcasts the event, while SQS stores it for processing.

Very simple rule
SNS → "Tell them"
SQS → "Give this to a worker to process"

For your previous example:

CPU > 80% → email 5 people

✅ CloudWatch → SNS → Emails

CPU > 80% → application should automatically process the event

✅ CloudWatch → SQS → Worker/Lambda




what will process 

The Worker/Consumer processes the message. It is a program, not a person.

For example, suppose SQS contains:

"Process Order #101"

The flow is:

SQS
 ↓
Worker / Consumer
 ↓
Reads "Process Order #101"
 ↓
Processes the order
 ↓
If successful → deletes message from SQS
What does "process order" mean?

It depends on what your application is programmed to do. For example:

Order message
     ↓
Worker
     ├── Check order details
     ├── Save order to database
     ├── Reduce product stock
     ├── Create invoice
     └── Send confirmation

The worker could be:

AWS Lambda
An application running on EC2
An application running on ECS
A Kubernetes application

So:

SQS stores the task → Worker takes the task → Worker performs the actual work → Worker deletes the message after success.

